From 8350bdb8a63888c606aadcb118d0c427b8d797cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Craterbrain <58347640+Craterbrain@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 20:43:46 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] soong_ui: plan the build from the memory that is free now

Soong sizes its parallelism from total RAM ("32 GB or less: 2 memory-heavy
jobs"), whatever else is running. On a 30 GB desktop that already holds 10+ GB
(idle pages swapped to zram), a full ROM build drove memory and swap past the
90% swap line at which systemd-oomd kills the whole build, four times in a row.

The build's memory budget is the available RAM (MemAvailable) minus
(100 - ceiling)% of the total (ceiling 88%, just under oomd's 90%) minus an
optional extra reserve. From it come the default -j (about 2.5 GiB per job,
never above cpus+2), the number of memory-heavy jobs (about 10 GiB each,
1 or 2), and a cap on soong_build's Go heap (85% of the budget, 8 to 24 GiB;
a smaller GOMEMLIMIT already set still wins). An explicit -j or
NINJA_HIGHMEM_NUM_JOBS always wins. The decision is printed at the start.

  PLAIT_MEM_BUDGET=off        stock behavior
  PLAIT_MEM_CEILING_PCT=88    the ceiling, 50..98
  PLAIT_MEM_RESERVE_GB=0      extra memory left to everything else

Linux only (/proc/meminfo); no change on macOS. The calculation is in
memory_budget.go with unit tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
